 What was wrong\n\nThe README claimed seven fault boundaries each carry four properties (digest path absent, no staged residue, nothing manufactured into quarantine, retry installs the declared bytes). Only three did. For the existing-quarantine-denied case two of the four are **false by design** — the corrupt object deliberately stays at the digest path and the candidate *is* preserved — and the very next sentence said so, so the section contradicted itself across two adjacent sentences. Same root cause as the miss against the front-loaded rule: both quarantine-ENOSPC cases are full-disk cases that asserted only that the call failed.\n\n## What I did\n\nRecovery halves added to six cases — the five the reviewer named plus the symlinked shard, so the rewritten README list has no member missing a property. The two quarantine-ENOSPC cases carry recovery in a *different shape*, and each now says so at the case: a candidate that was never a valid object can't install on retry, so its recovery is that it's preserved as evidence; a corrupt existing object is deliberately left in place, so its recovery is **what the next run does with it still there** — it reaches `ErrImmutableConflict`, quarantines both artifacts, frees the digest path, and a third run installs. That path was entirely unpinned.\n\nNo production file changed this round. `object_store.go`, `projection.go` and the two accepted test files are byte-identical to the reviewed candidate.\n\n## Evidence\n\n5 mutants, 5 killed, each run scoped with `-run` so only the named test can kill it, restored from a byte-for-byte backup with the restore sha256-verified. Three of them matter — each leaves the **faulted first half passing** and dies only at a line added this round:\n\n| Mutant | Killed at |\n| --- | --- |\n| M5 — existing artifact moved, path never reported | `object_store_fault_test.go:490` |\n| M6 — candidate quarantined, path dropped from result | `:434` |\n| M4 — genuine absence classified as unsafe entry | all six retry halves: `:247`, `:291`, `:366` ×2, `_unix_test.go:109`, `:147` |\n\nM1/M2 also died but at pre-existing lines — whole-test binds, not recovery binds, and I've reported them as such.\n\nTwo honesty notes I'd rather state than have you find: the `assertQuarantineIsEmpty` I added to the group-accessible-shard case is a state assertion backing a README claim, **not** a gate proof — that refusal returns before anything is staged, so no natural mutant can put something in quarantine there. And I did not rerun the five fuzz targets, the JSON sweep, or `task-board validate`; none of them reads the two `_test.go` files, README or LOGBOOK that I touched, so round-1's results stand.\n\nEverything else ran as a standalone process, all exit 0: gofmt, build, vet, `go test ./...`, `-race`, `-cover`, tracecheck, `cataloggen -check`, both cross-compile targets, `git diff --check`. `internal/localstore` unchanged at 86.6%, 90 tests / 130 subtests — assertions added to existing cases, zero new `t.Run`.\n\nOne correction worth flagging: I initially wrote \"122 → 130 subtests\" into the LOGBOOK, which was fabricated — no subtests were added. Verified and rewritten to state the count is unchanged.\n\nChanges left uncommitted for the Story checkpoint, matching round 1 and the story-level commit pattern in the log. Board is at `to-review`, 15/15, with the rework report and mutation transcript attached.","ttft_ms":2110,"type":"result","duration_ms":849034,"uuid":"ca8e7e93-aa53-4146-a478-38697a8ca172","ttft_stream_ms":1364,"time_to_request_ms":33,"queued_turn_count":0}
